<p>Google's site reputation abuse policy got another important update on August 28, 2026. The headline is not that Google abandoned the policy. It changed how manual-action enforcement works for searches in the European Economic Area (EEA).</p>
<h2>What did Google change in August 2026?</h2><p>Starting August 30, 2026, a site-reputation manual action has different effects by region. Outside the EEA, the affected section can continue to be directly impacted in Search. For users inside the EEA, the manual action does not directly apply to Search results; Google says the affected section may instead be separated in its systems so it can rank independently over time.</p>
<h2>What is site reputation abuse?</h2><p>Google defines site reputation abuse as publishing third-party content mainly to take advantage of a host site's established ranking signals. The policy is aimed at manipulative use of a trusted domain, not at every page that happens to be produced by a partner or outside contributor.</p>
<h2>Does first-party involvement make third-party content safe?</h2><p>Google's November 2024 policy update said that cooperation, licensing, white-label arrangements or partial ownership do not by themselves change the fundamental third-party nature of content when it is being used to exploit a host site's ranking signals. The site and the purpose of the content still matter.</p>
<h2>What changed for sites with global audiences?</h2><p>Regional enforcement now matters. One affected section can have a different Search outcome for EEA users than for users elsewhere. That makes international traffic analysis important: a single country filter can hide a policy-related change that looks small in global reporting.</p>
<h2>How should publishers reduce site-reputation risk?</h2><p>Keep a clear editorial reason for hosting outside content. Document who creates it, who reviews it, why it belongs on the site, and whether the section exists primarily because the host domain already ranks well. Avoid turning your strongest domain into a container for unrelated commercial content simply because it can inherit existing visibility.</p>
<h2>What happens if Google applies a manual action?</h2><p>Google says Search Console will notify site owners when a manual action is applied. Publishers can request reconsideration when they believe the action was incorrect or after they have corrected the issue. Google also notes that moving policy-violating content does not automatically solve the problem.</p>
<h2>Is every guest post or partner page a policy violation?</h2><p>No. The policy focuses on third-party content published mainly to exploit the host site's reputation. A genuine editorial partnership can still exist, but publishers should be able to explain the content's purpose and the value it gives the audience without relying on the host site's ranking strength as the main business case.</p>
<h2>Why this matters for SEO strategy in 2026</h2><p>The safest approach is to build topical sections that make sense for the site's users and editorial mission. Google has also clarified that its spam policies apply to generative AI responses in Search, so AI-generated content does not create a loophole around the same quality and spam rules.</p>
